CH = dict(num=12, title="Understanding Markets", tag="Economics", theme="Markets, price, supply chain, government role, consumer awareness",
 intro="Almost everything we use reaches us through a market. This chapter explains what a market is, how prices are settled, the kinds of markets, how goods travel from producer to consumer, what markets mean for society, how the government regulates them and how consumers can judge quality.",
 big_questions=["What are markets and how do they function?", "What is the role of markets in people's lives?", "What role does government play in markets?", "How can consumers assess quality of goods and services?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "12.1 What Is a Market?"),
 ("ul", ["A **market** is a place (physical or online) where people buy and sell goods and services; also called *bazaar*, *haat* (Hindi), *marukatte* (Kannada).",
  "A **need** is essential for survival (food, water, clothing, shelter); a **want** is desired but not essential. Markets help meet both, and also connect people, traditions and ideas.",
  "**Trade** = buying and selling or exchange of goods and services between people or countries."]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**Hampi Bazaar** (Vijayanagara Empire, opposite the Virupaksha temple): Portuguese travellers **Domingos Paes** ('the best-provided city in the world') and **Fernao Nuniz** described abundant grain, oil, silk, animals, birds, jewels, rubies, diamonds, pearls and cloth, and craftsmen working in the streets."),
 ("h2", "12.2 Prices and Markets"),
 ("ul", ["Every market has a **buyer and a seller** who must agree on a **price** (amount at which a buyer is willing to buy and a seller willing to sell). They often **negotiate or bargain**.",
  "Guava example: the seller asks ₹80/kg — too high, buyers refuse; ₹20/kg — too low, seller makes a loss; about **₹40/kg** — acceptable to both. With experience the seller learns how many guavas to bring.",
  "Over time the quantity sellers offer (**supply**) and the quantity buyers want (**demand**) settle a price that is high enough for the seller and low enough for the buyer. **Demand** = quantity consumers are willing and able to buy at a price; **Supply** = quantity sellers are willing and able to sell at a price.",
  "Examples of price change: vegetables become cheaper late at night at weekly markets (perishable, avoid loss); heavy discounts on woollens at the end of winter (unsold stock)."]),
 ("h2", "12.3 Types of Markets"),
 ("table", ["Basis", "Type", "Features / examples"], [
  ["Place", "Physical market", "Buyers and sellers meet in person: weekly markets and haats, local shops, street food stalls, malls; services needing contact (tailoring, salon) need physical presence"],
  ["Place", "Online market", "Apps / websites; goods delivered to the door; services like online classes; payment online; manufacturers can buy inputs online"],
  ["Other", "Stock market", "Deals in shares, not goods and services (studied later)"],
  ["Geography", "Domestic market", "Within the country's boundaries (e.g., paper bought from mills in India)"],
  ["Geography", "International market", "Across borders: **exports** (selling abroad) and **imports** (buying from abroad)"],
  ["Quantity", "Wholesale market", "Bulk buying and selling (mandis, Khari Baoli spice market in Delhi, flower market Bengaluru)"],
  ["Quantity", "Retail market", "Small quantities to final consumers (garment shops, salons, groceries, Johari Bazaar Jaipur)"]]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "India's trade (from the map): exports include outsourced services (software) to North America, chemical products to South America, pharmaceuticals to Africa, engineering goods to Europe, refined petroleum to West Asia. Imports include aircraft and parts from North America, copper ores from South America, diamonds from Africa, electrical equipment from Europe, crude oil and fertilisers from West Asia, vegetable oils from Southeast Asia. In 2024 India was the world's largest importer of vegetable oils (palm oil mainly from Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand)."),
 ("h2", "12.4 The Chain of Supply"),
 ("ol", ["**Producer / manufacturer** (farm or factory; an **input** is a material used to produce goods).",
  "**Wholesaler**: buys in bulk from producers (e.g., farms), stores in **godowns** (warehouses) or **cold storage** for perishables, sells in **mandis** and wholesale markets.",
  "**Distributor**: supplies goods from manufacturers / wholesalers to retailers, bridging distance and difficult terrain (like milk middlemen in the AMUL story).",
  "**Retailer**: shop near households selling small quantities to **consumers** for consumption, not resale.",
  "In **online markets**: manufacturers send bulk goods to an **aggregator** (website / app combining offers of many sellers), which packs and delivers to the buyer."]),
 ("h3", "Case study: Surat"),
 ("ul", ["Asia's oldest textile market; thousands of factories making cotton and synthetic textiles. **Raw cotton** arrives through cotton mandis from Maharashtra and other parts of Gujarat; goes through weaving (power looms), dyeing and finishing, with a market at each stage; finished sarees and garments are sold through wholesalers to small shops and big stores in India and abroad. Wholesalers also gauge retailers' demand, keeping supply smooth.",
  "Surat also has the world's largest **diamond** industry (about 1.5 million artisans cutting and polishing). Trade has flourished since the **16th century** thanks to its west-coast location (port), roads, railways and generations of skilled artisans."]),
 ("h2", "12.5 Role of Markets in People's Lives"),
 ("ul", ["Markets link producers and consumers and give access to what people cannot produce themselves; without them farmers could not sell grain nor cloth makers get cotton.",
  "Some products lack a ready market (e.g., artist Aakriti's paintings: few buyers and sellers, so price and buyers are hard to find).",
  "**Signals to producers**: if many consumers ask for energy-saving refrigerators, producers make them, benefiting society.",
  "**Non-economic role**: long-term trust between buyers and sellers (tailor, jeweller, grocer's monthly account); markets mix cultures. **Ima Keithal** (Mother's Market) in Imphal, Manipur, is run by about **3,000 women**, selling vegetables, Manipuri attire, handloom and handicrafts; gives employment and is a cultural meeting place. South Indian haldi-kumkum sellers give a little free as a mark of good wishes."]),
 ("h2", "12.6 Government's Role in Markets"),
 ("table", ["Role", "How it works"], [
  ["Controlling prices", "Maximum price on essentials like life-saving drugs; **minimum price** (support price) for wheat, paddy, maize to protect farmers; **minimum wages**. Too low a price discourages producers; too high hurts consumers"],
  ["Quality and safety standards", "Approval and sample testing of medicines; standards for goods and services; checks on weights and measures (net quantity). Kautilya's *Arthashastra* asked ghee sellers to add 1/50 extra (*manasrava*) to compensate for ghee sticking to the container"],
  ["Reducing external effects", "Regulations on pollution, single-use plastics, health risks; too many rules can hamper markets"],
  ["Providing public goods", "Parks, roads, policing, welfare services where profit is not expected; available to all, use today does not reduce availability tomorrow"]]),
 ("h2", "12.7 Assessing Quality: Consumer Awareness"),
 ("ul", ["Read the label on a packet: net quantity, date of manufacture, batch number, best-before date, **MRP** (maximum retail price), manufacturer's name and address, nutrition facts, ingredients, allergen declaration, **FSSAI** mark and licence number."]),
 ("table", ["Mark", "Issued by", "Used on"], [
  ["FSSAI", "Food Safety and Standards Authority of India", "Food products: safe to consume"],
  ["ISI mark", "Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS)", "Electrical appliances, construction material, tyres, paper; safe and good quality"],
  ["AGMARK", "Government (Ag = agriculture)", "Vegetables, fruits, cereals, pulses, spices, honey"],
  ["BEE Star rating", "Bureau of Energy Efficiency", "TVs, laptops, ACs, refrigerators: more stars means less electricity"]]),
 ("p", "Besides certification, buyers rely on a product's **reputation** (word of mouth) and **online reviews**."),
 ("h2", "12.8 Quick Revision"),
 ("qa", [("What is a market?", "A place, physical or online, where buyers and sellers exchange goods and services for money."),
  ("Distinguish need and want.", "A need is essential for survival; a want is desired but not essential."),
  ("How is price determined?", "Through bargaining and the interaction of buyers' demand and sellers' supply, until both agree."),
  ("Define demand and supply.", "Demand: quantity buyers are willing and able to buy at a price; supply: quantity sellers are willing and able to sell."),
  ("Differentiate physical and online markets.", "Physical: buyers and sellers meet in person; online: transactions via apps and websites from anywhere."),
  ("Distinguish domestic and international markets.", "Domestic trade stays within the country; international trade crosses borders through exports and imports."),
  ("Who are wholesalers and retailers?", "Wholesalers buy and sell in bulk; retailers sell small quantities to final consumers."),
  ("What does an aggregator do?", "An online business combining many sellers; it packs and delivers goods to buyers."),
  ("Why is Surat a trading hub?", "Cotton supply, power looms and dyeing units, wholesale network, port, roads, railways and skilled artisans since the 16th century."),
  ("How do markets benefit society?", "Give access to goods, send demand signals to producers, provide employment and bring people together (e.g. Ima Keithal)."),
  ("How does the government regulate markets?", "Sets price limits, minimum wages and support prices; ensures quality and safety; controls pollution; supplies public goods."),
  ("What do FSSAI, ISI, AGMARK and BEE Star show?", "Food safety; quality of industrial goods; quality of farm produce; energy efficiency."),
  ("What is a public good? Give examples.", "A service available to all not provided for profit, e.g. roads, parks, policing.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["A **market** brings buyers and sellers together; price comes from **demand and supply** and negotiation.",
  "Types: **physical / online, domestic / international, wholesale / retail**.",
  "Chain: **producer → wholesaler → (distributor) → retailer → consumer**; online uses an **aggregator**.",
  "Markets also build relationships and culture (Hampi Bazaar, Ima Keithal).",
  "Government controls prices, ensures standards, limits harm and provides **public goods**.",
  "Consumers can check **FSSAI, ISI, AGMARK, BEE Star**, labels and reviews."])
